we use adaptor pattern when we have an existing class
we want to use it somewhere,
but the interface of this class does not match the form that we expect.
(and we can't change the type!)

in this situation we can use adaptor pattern to convert the interface
to different form!
we can create a middle class that work as adaptor,
has the type that we want,
and using the code that library has!
we create this class as "CaramelAdapter"

Note: we can use inheritance to use adapter pattern (Mosh2)
      extend to Caramel to access his code
      and implement the Filter interface to has the type,
      but due to java limit classes to extend just one class we have some limitation here!
      (every class just has one parent)
      so using composition is more flexible than inheritance.
      The old saying "favor composition over inheritance!"
      inheritance is not bad, but sometime composition is better solution.

Note: if Filter was abstract class instead interface
      we can't use inheritance
      (we need to extend it to both Filter & Caramel)

Note: because of this limitation "Composition" is better approach than "Inheritance"
      and GOF using composition!

Note:  Proxy says:
       “I’m exactly like the real object, but before reaching the real object, go through me.”
       (THEY USE SAME INTERFACE)
       it's like proxy on servers, they want to hide main server!
       Adapter says:
       “This class doesn’t have the interface you want; I translate it so it becomes usable.”

